        "original_id": "53d0caa1-cc9a-57d3-8c80-0b6062fa0d59",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Barry Werth, Joel White, Joshua Boger, E. B. White, Katharine Sergeant Angell White\n- Organizations: The New York Times, The New Yorker, GQ, Smithsonian, MIT Technology Review, Vertex Pharmaceuticals\n- Products: The Billion-Dollar Molecule, W-Class boats\n- Topics: publishing, biotechnology, rational drug design, naval architecture\n\nFacts:\n- In 1925, The New Yorker started as a weekly magazine.\n- The New Yorker is now published 47 times annually, and five of its issues cover two-week spans.\n- Joel White was born in 1930 and was the son of author E. B. White and New Yorker editor Katharine Sergeant Angell White.\n- Joel White was a U.S. naval architect known for classic designs including the W-Class of boats.\n- Vertex Pharmaceuticals was founded in 1989 by Joshua Boger.\n- Vertex Pharmaceuticals was among the first biotechnology companies to adopt an explicit strategy of rational drug design rather than techniques based on combinatorial chemistry.\n- The Billion-Dollar Molecule is a book by journalist Barry Werth about the founding and early research efforts of Vertex Pharmaceuticals.\n- Barry Werth's work has appeared in The New York Times, The New Yorker, GQ, Smithsonian, and MIT Technology Review.\n- Joel White died in 1997.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "85cd82c8-a36a-5885-8d0f-5143e514f31c",
        "text": "Page: Barry Werth. Sentence: His work has appeared in \"The New York Times\", \"The New Yorker\", \"GQ\", the \"Smithsonian\", and the \"MIT Technology Review\".\nPage: The New Yorker. Sentence: Started as a weekly in 1925, the magazine is now published 47 times annually, with five of these issues covering two-week spans.\nPage: The Billion-Dollar Molecule. Sentence: The Billion-Dollar Molecule is a book by journalist Barry Werth about the founding and early research efforts of the American biotechnology company Vertex Pharmaceuticals, which was founded in 1989 by Joshua Boger and was among the first biotechnology companies to adopt an explicit strategy of rational drug design as opposed to techniques based on combinatorial chemistry.\nPage: Joel White. Sentence: Joel White (1930–1997), the son of author E. B. White and New Yorker Magazine editor Katharine Sergeant Angell White, was a U.S. naval architect known for his classic designs including the W-Class of boats.",
